 "notes_for_editor": "Early history (1870-74 appropriations, construction, 1874 lens, 1952 lens removal, 1962 fire, 1936 land transfer) rests on LighthouseFriends alone; the 1870s Light-House Board reports are not in the local corpus. The 1853 vs 1874 first-light question is unresolved: the HAER form and DNR say 1853, LighthouseFriends puts the 1853 light at Two Rivers. LighthouseFriends gives Allen's term as 1889-95; the roster says 1888-95. The 1897 Life-Saving report's table columns are scrambled by OCR, so the vessel (possibly the schooner Walhalla) and exact date are not named. Joseph Napiezinski and Joseph Kimmes careers are joined by name at one station only. 1919 diaphone: the Bulletin entry is undated within the year; LighthouseFriends gives 23 Sept 1919. Wisconsin newspapers via Chronicling America returned no hits. Fact-check 2026-10-22: dates 17 July 1890 and 9 Nov 1893 are read from OCR text (not page images). Chicago River discontinuance date appears as '1803' in the OCR and is read as 1893. The 1897 LSS entry's vessel and date are scrambled in the OCR table, so the story leaves them out. Roster careers joined by name at one station only.",
